pastex (1.10.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Never leave the window minimized, and stop skipping Ctrl+V on a slow
    minimize. The hide check gave up after 300 ms, but Cinnamon animates the
    minimize for a few hundred milliseconds; when the window finished hiding
    just after the check, Pastex had already decided "never hidden", sent no
    Ctrl+V and left the window minimized. The check now allows 600 ms.
  * Restore the window instead of giving up on it. The branch that concluded
    "the window never hid" did not deiconify anything, so a minimize that
    completed a moment later was permanent. It now asks for the window back
    and logs the observed state.
  * Verify the restore. deiconify() is dropped when it arrives while the
    minimize animation is still running, which also left the window minimized
    for good. The state is now re-read every 60 ms for up to 1.2 s and the
    request is repeated until the window is really back, in every path
    (success, failure, watchdog deadline and the give-up case).
  * Do not overwrite a running paste with the manual hint. When the <Unmap>
    signal released Ctrl+V before the copy worker reported back, the copy
    result replaced the status with "press Ctrl+V" even though the sender was
    already running; a _paste_started flag keeps that path quiet.
  * Seven new tests and three new mutation checks cover the restore path.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.10.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the package Description that shipped in 1.10.0. The control file is
    generated by an unquoted shell heredoc, so the backticked
    pastex --enable-permissions inside the description was run as a command
    substitution and replaced by empty output: "apt show pastex" said "and
    only" followed by "installs them.". The heredoc is now quoted and only the
    @PKG@, @VERSION@ and @ARCH@ placeholders are substituted, and the
    description no longer contains backticks or "$" at all.
  * Two tests render the generated control file and reject shell
    metacharacters in any description, and two mutation checks cover both the
    quoting and the text.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.10.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop configuring the Wayland helpers when the package is installed. Debian
    postinst used to create the 'pastex' group, add the invoking account to it
    (which needs a logout), reload the udev rules and enable a per-user
    ydotoold unit on every install, even though Linux Mint's default X11
    session needs none of it. It now only prints a one-time note pointing at
    `pastex --enable-permissions`, and the executing account is never touched.
  * Ship the optional Wayland payload as inert templates. The udev rule and the
    ydotoold user unit moved from /usr/lib/udev/rules.d and /usr/lib/systemd/
    user to /usr/lib/pastex; only `pastex --enable-permissions` (the existing
    pkexec helper) copies the rule into place, adds the group membership and
    enables the unit. A package upgrade therefore cannot change device
    permissions by itself.
  * Report the X11 session as needing no permissions anywhere in the UI. The
    pastex group and the /dev/uinput rows, the "Next steps" advice and the
    CLI hint no longer appear in `--check`, `--permissions` or the paste
    failure dialog on X11; they are replaced by a single line stating that
    xdotool types through XTEST. The paste failure dialog also stops blaming
    /dev/uinput there and points at focus instead.
  * Move the Wayland-only tool recommendations. Recommends is now xclip, xsel
    and xdotool, so `apt install pastex` on X11 no longer drags in wl-clipboard,
    ydotool and python3-dbus; those three are Suggests. A test keeps every
    clipboard tool the code calls declared in one of the two fields.
  * Fix the copy failure message: it no longer mentions the internal clipboard
    fallback that was removed in 1.4.0, and it names the Mint packages
    (xclip/xsel/xdotool) instead of wl-clipboard only.
  * Five new mutation checks keep the X11 behaviour from regressing.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.9.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Target Linux Mint only. Pastex now documents, packages and tests for Linux
    Mint (Cinnamon, MATE and Xfce; X11 by default and the Cinnamon Wayland
    session) instead of Ubuntu with a GNOME-first PPA. The Ubuntu PPA
    workflow and the apt-repo/pastex.list.example file are gone; the only
    supported installation is APT from the signed archive.
  * Rename the APT archive suite from "stonking" to "mint". The suite is a
    fixed label for this project, not a Mint release codename, so the same
    architecture-independent package serves every Mint edition and release.
    A sources file written by 1.8.x still names "stonking"; re-running the
    README installation block, or replacing that suite name with "mint", is
    enough to keep upgrading (the keyring does not change).
  * Rewrite the README installation section to match the youtube repository:
    one subshell that downloads the keyring into a temporary directory, prints
    the 40-character fingerprint for confirmation, writes the deb822
    .sources file with Signed-By, and then documents upgrade, removal and
    local package installation, including the expected
    "Hit: ... mint InRelease" line.
  * Say the default session first: on X11 automatic paste needs only xclip and
    xdotool, with no /dev/uinput, no pastex group and permission checks that
    are reported as not needed. The Wayland clipboard owner
    (ext-data-control-v1) is described as applying only to compositors that
    implement it, and panel wording replaces dock wording throughout.
  * Translate every remaining Indonesian string into English: the startup line
    is now "[paste] ready: copy=... paste=... ydotoold=running|not-running",
    the clipboard abort message reads "aborted: the window was already
    restored", and the paste log lines, code comments and test messages are
    English.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.8.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix one-click paste doing nothing on GNOME. The log said "window manager
    did not minimize (state=normal)", but iconify() only *asks* the compositor:
    the window is still reported as "normal" for a few milliseconds, so Pastex
    concluded the minimize had failed and copied the text without ever sending
    Ctrl+V. The state is now polled in 20 ms steps for up to 300 ms, the
    minimize request is repeated once at 120 ms for compositors that ignore
    the first one, and the <Unmap> signal still sends Ctrl+V as soon as the
    window really hides. Only a window that never hides keeps the manual
    Ctrl+V hint.
  * Report the daemon in the startup line: "siap: salin=... tempel=...
    ydotoold=jalan|tidak-jalan", so a missing ydotoold is visible before the
    first click instead of only after it.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 17 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.8.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Never leave the window minimized when Ctrl+V cannot be delivered. A click
    now arms a 9 s watchdog: if the send stage overruns it, the window comes
    back, the remaining tools are abandoned (so no key press can land in
    Pastex afterwards) and the failure dialog says why. Every tool call in the
    send stage now has its own timeout (2 s per keystroke, 6 s for the portal
    consent dialog), and an exception in either worker thread is reported
    instead of leaving the window hidden.
  * Start a private ydotoold when the user unit is missing. Ubuntu ships
    ydotoold.service as a system unit, so "systemctl --user start
    ydotoold.service" fails on a stock install and automatic paste never
    worked; Pastex now starts ydotoold itself with a socket it owns in
    XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and points ydotool at it through YDOTOOL_SOCKET. Only
    /dev/uinput access is still required, which --enable-permissions sets up.
  * Stop hiding the window once a paste has failed: the next click copies the
    text, keeps the window visible and shows the manual Ctrl+V hint, instead
    of minimizing and immediately bouncing back.
  * verify-regressions runs pytest with PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE so a cached
    .pyc can no longer make a restored bug look like a passing test.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 17 Nov 2026 06:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Optional clipboard owner: own the Wayland selection through
    ext-data-control-v1 instead of spawning wl-copy for every copy. Opt in
    with "pastex --wayland-clipboard"; the choice is saved. The protocol layer
    runs in a separate helper process, only becomes the copy path after a
    nonce/read-back self-test, and every failure (no data-control support, no
    read-back, helper exit) falls back to wl-copy for that copy and then for
    the session. Copies that use it no longer pay the 5-20 ms fork/connect of
    wl-copy. "--check" reports whether it is available and enabled.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 16 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

pastex (1.7.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Repaint the tree by patching rows instead of deleting and rebuilding it:
    unchanged rows are only compared, changed rows are rewritten in place, and
    the scroll position, the expanded folders and the selection survive a
    refresh. The two earlier passes over the index (insert, then reorder) no
    longer rebuild every Text item on each keystroke or inotify event.
  * Drop the --export-data option and the built-in sample snippets that backed
    it. The package never bundled sample data; the GUI asks for a source folder
    instead, and "--list" now points at --dir rather than a removed command.
    Samples that older installs already have stay untouched.

